---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import RecordImage from '../components/RecordImage.astro';
---

<Base
  title="The Story of Soke Michael Kinney — Soke Kinney Memorial Fund"
  description="The life of Soke Michael Kinney: martial artist, teacher, and community leader, from Silver Spring, Maryland to St. Petersburg, Florida."
>

  <div class="story-hero">
    <div class="story-hero__inner">
      <p class="eyebrow">His story</p>
      <h1 class="story-title">Soke Michael Kinney</h1>
      <p class="story-dates">1952 – 2014</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <article class="story-body" aria-label="Biography of Soke Michael Kinney">

    <!-- ── 1952 Born ─────────────────────────────────────── -->
    <section class="decade fade-rise" aria-labelledby="era-born">
      <div class="decade__marker">
        <span class="decade__year">1952</span>
        <span class="decade__rule" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      </div>
      <div class="decade__content">
        <h2 id="era-born" class="decade__heading">Born</h2>
        <p class="decade__prose">
          Michael Kinney was born in 1952. He would go on to spend more than fifty years
          in the martial arts — not as a competitor building a trophy case, but as a teacher
          building a community. That distinction mattered to him, and it shaped everything
          that followed.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ── 1963 Begins training ───────────────────────────── -->
    <section class="decade fade-rise" data-delay="60" aria-labelledby="era-1963">
      <div class="decade__marker">
        <span class="decade__year">1963</span>
        <span class="decade__rule" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      </div>
      <div class="decade__content">
        <h2 id="era-1963" class="decade__heading">The beginning, Silver Spring</h2>
        <p class="decade__prose">
          In 1963, at eleven years old, Michael began training in Silver Spring, Maryland —
          first in Ju-Jitsu, then in Tang Soo Do. His first instructor was
          Grand Master Ki Whang Kim, born in Seoul in 1920, who had founded the Tang Soo Do
          Karate Association in the United States and served as chairman of the US Olympic
          Taekwondo committee. Training under a teacher of that standing, from the very
          beginning, shaped the seriousness with which Michael would approach the art
          for the rest of his life.
        </p>
        <p class="decade__prose">
          The following year his father died. His mother, working three jobs to keep the
          family together, arranged for him to continue lessons at a lower cost through one
          of Mr. Kim's black belts at the local YMCA. She understood what it meant to him.
          He never forgot that.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ── 1963–1969 Brown belt ──────────────────────────── -->
    <section class="decade fade-rise" data-delay="60" aria-labelledby="era-brown">
      <div class="decade__marker">
        <span class="decade__year">1963–1969</span>
        <span class="decade__rule" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      </div>
      <div class="decade__content">
        <h2 id="era-brown" class="decade__heading">Six years at brown belt</h2>
        <p class="decade__prose">
          There was no junior black belt rank in the Tang Soo Do Karate Association at
          the time. A student could not test for black belt until the age of eighteen.
          Michael held his brown belt for the better part of six years — not because he
          wasn't ready, but because the organization required him to wait. He waited.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ── 1969 First TKA black belt ─────────────────────── -->
    <section class="decade fade-rise" data-delay="60" aria-labelledby="era-1969">
      <div class="decade__marker">
        <span class="decade__year">1969</span>
        <span class="decade__rule" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      </div>
      <div class="decade__content">
        <h2 id="era-1969" class="decade__heading">The first TKA black belt</h2>
        <p class="decade__prose">
          In 1969, at eighteen, Michael Kinney received the first Tang Soo Do Karate
          Association black belt, studying under instructor Dale Tompkins. He immediately
          became the chief black belt instructor of the new school — a position he was
          already prepared for, having spent years in the art while waiting for the rank
          to catch up to his training.
        </p>
        <div class="decade__image">
          <RecordImage
            src="/images/michaelandkid3.jpg"
            alt="Michael Kinney in a training session, early years"
            nativeWidth={206}
            nativeHeight={325}
            year="1960s–70s"
            location="Maryland"
            source="Kinney Family"
          />
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ── 1970s TKA growth ───────────────────────────────── -->
    <section class="decade fade-rise" data-delay="60" aria-labelledby="era-1970s">
      <div class="decade__marker">
        <span class="decade__year">1970s</span>
        <span class="decade__rule" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      </div>
      <div class="decade__content">
        <h2 id="era-1970s" class="decade__heading">Building the association</h2>
        <p class="decade__prose">
          Through the early 1970s the Tang Soo Do Karate Association grew rapidly under
          Michael's involvement, reaching more than fifty locations and over two thousand
          students by 1975. The TKA was among the first martial arts organizations to
          contract directly with recreation centers and school gymnasiums — bringing
          instruction to students who might not have found their way to a traditional
          dojo. That model, of going to where the community was rather than waiting for
          the community to come to you, would define his teaching for decades.
        </p>
        <p class="decade__prose">
          From 1968 through 1980 he competed on the east coast tournament circuit. From
          1970 through 1980 he coached and competed on the TKA team, which went undefeated
          for nine consecutive years. He also co-promoted the Eastern Regional Karate
          Championships, which debuted in 1970.
        </p>
        <div class="decade__image">
          <RecordImage
            src="/images/image-asset__1_.jpeg"
            alt="Soke Michael Kinney with students, St. Petersburg era"
            nativeWidth={960}
            nativeHeight={637}
            year="1970s–80s"
            source="Kinney Family"
          />
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ── 1980 St. Petersburg ────────────────────────────── -->
    <section class="decade fade-rise" data-delay="60" aria-labelledby="era-1980">
      <div class="decade__marker">
        <span class="decade__year">1980</span>
        <span class="decade__rule" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      </div>
      <div class="decade__content">
        <h2 id="era-1980" class="decade__heading">St. Petersburg</h2>
        <p class="decade__prose">
          In 1980, when his parents retired to St. Petersburg, Florida, Michael followed.
          The city's Recreation Department offered him a small back room to teach in.
          Within three months the program had outgrown the room and taken over the gym.
          It later expanded city-wide.
        </p>
        <p class="decade__prose">
          Kinney Karate ran the city recreation department's program for more than thirty
          years. That sustained partnership — classes in rec centers, schools, and
          community gymnasiums — was credited as one of the factors that helped
          St. Petersburg earn a national All-American City award.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ── 1980s–2010s Honors and television ─────────────── -->
    <section class="decade fade-rise" data-delay="60" aria-labelledby="era-honors">
      <div class="decade__marker">
        <span class="decade__year">1980s–2010s</span>
        <span class="decade__rule" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      </div>
      <div class="decade__content">
        <h2 id="era-honors" class="decade__heading">Recognition</h2>
        <p class="decade__prose">
          Over the following decades Soke Kinney's work attracted national attention.
          He appeared on NBC's <em>Today Show</em> and ABC's <em>Wide World of Sports</em>,
          and hosted <em>Kinney Karate World</em>, produced by Group W. His instruction
          and his program were covered in <em>Black Belt</em>, <em>Karate Illustrated</em>,
          <em>Professional Karate</em>, <em>Inside Kung Fu</em>,
          <em>U.S. News and World Report</em>, and the <em>Washington Post Magazine</em>.
        </p>
        <p class="decade__prose">
          He received a national volunteer award from President Obama — recognition that
          situated his decades of community work in a context beyond martial arts. By
          the end of his life, Kinney Karate operated nine locations with more than six
          hundred students. He held a 10th Degree Black Belt. The title <em>Soke</em>
          designates the founder and head of a martial arts system.
        </p>
        <div class="decade__images-grid">
          <RecordImage
            src="/images/image-asset__9.jpeg"
            alt="Formal portrait of Soke Michael Kinney"
            nativeWidth={300}
            nativeHeight={392}
            source="Kinney Family"
          />
          <RecordImage
            src="/images/image-asset__3_.jpeg"
            alt="Soke Michael Kinney with students"
            nativeWidth={300}
            nativeHeight={261}
            source="Kinney Family"
          />
          <RecordImage
            src="/images/image-asset__10_.jpeg"
            alt="Soke Michael Kinney at an event"
            nativeWidth={300}
            nativeHeight={225}
            source="Kinney Family"
          />
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ── 2014 ───────────────────────────────────────────── -->
    <section class="decade fade-rise" data-delay="60" aria-labelledby="era-2014">
      <div class="decade__marker">
        <span class="decade__year">2014</span>
        <span class="decade__rule" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      </div>
      <div class="decade__content">
        <h2 id="era-2014" class="decade__heading">October 6, 2014</h2>
        <p class="decade__prose">
          Soke Michael Kinney died on October 6, 2014, in his sleep, at the age of sixty-two.
          A celebration of his life was held on October 19, 2014, at the Northwest Community
          Center in St. Petersburg — the same kind of place where so much of his work
          had been done.
        </p>
        <p class="decade__prose">
          He taught tens of thousands of students over more than fifty years. More than a
          thousand of them earned black belts. The fund that bears his name exists to make
          sure that the door he held open stays open.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

  </article>

  <!-- ── Photo invitation ───────────────────────────────── -->
  <aside class="photo-invite fade-rise" aria-labelledby="photos-heading">
    <div class="photo-invite__inner">
      <p class="eyebrow" style="color: var(--crimson);">Help us preserve his history</p>
      <h2 id="photos-heading" class="photo-invite__heading">Do you have photographs of Soke Kinney?</h2>
      <p class="photo-invite__body">
        The photographs on this page were gathered from students, family members,
        and old albums — and we know there are more out there. If you trained with
        Soke Kinney, attended a tournament, or knew him in any capacity and have
        photographs you'd be willing to share, we'd be honored to hear from you.
        Please use the <strong>Photos of Soke Kinney</strong> topic in the contact
        form, and tell us what you have.
      </p>
      <a href="/contact?topic=photos" class="btn-primary">Share a photograph</a>
    </div>
  </aside>

</Base>

<style>
  /* ── Story hero ─────────────────────────────────────────── */
  .story-hero {
    background-color: var(--ink);
    padding-block: 4rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--ink-2);
  }

  .story-hero__inner {
    max-width: 1200px;
    margin-inline: auto;
    padding-inline: 1.5rem;
  }

  .eyebrow {
    font-family: 'Space Mono', 'Courier New', monospace;
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.14em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: rgba(244, 242, 236, 0.5);
    margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
    display: block;
  }

  .story-title {
    font-family: 'Newsreader Variable', 'Newsreader', Georgia, serif;
    font-size: clamp(2.25rem, 4.5vw, 3.5rem);
    font-weight: 400;
    color: var(--text-invert);
    line-height: 1.1;
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
  }

  .story-dates {
    font-family: 'Space Mono', 'Courier New', monospace;
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.2em;
    color: var(--gold);
  }

  /* ── Timeline body ──────────────────────────────────────── */
  .story-body {
    max-width: 1200px;
    margin-inline: auto;
    padding-inline: 1.5rem;
    padding-block: 4rem;
  }

  /* ── Decade entry ───────────────────────────────────────── */
  .decade {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 10rem 1fr;
    gap: 0 3rem;
    padding-block: 3rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
  }

  .decade:first-child {
    border-top: none;
  }

  .decade__marker {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: flex-end;
    padding-top: 0.2rem;
    gap: 0.75rem;
  }

  .decade__year {
    font-family: 'Space Mono', 'Courier New', monospace;
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    color: var(--crimson);
    text-align: right;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  .decade__rule {
    display: block;
    width: 2px;
    flex: 1;
    background-color: var(--line);
    min-height: 1.5rem;
  }

  .decade__content {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 1rem;
  }

  .decade__heading {
    font-family: 'Newsreader Variable', 'Newsreader', Georgia, serif;
    font-size: 1.35rem;
    font-weight: 500;
    color: var(--text);
    line-height: 1.3;
    margin-bottom: 0.25rem;
  }

  .decade__prose {
    font-family: 'Barlow', system-ui, sans-serif;
    font-size: 1.0625rem;
    line-height: 1.75;
    color: var(--text);
    max-width: 68ch;
  }

  .decade__image {
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
  }

  .decade__images-grid {
    display: flex;
    gap: 1.5rem;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
  }

  /* ── Photo invitation ───────────────────────────────────── */
  .photo-invite {
    background-color: var(--paper-2);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
    padding-block: 4rem;
  }

  .photo-invite__inner {
    max-width: 68ch;
    margin-inline: auto;
    padding-inline: 1.5rem;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 1rem;
  }

  .photo-invite__heading {
    font-family: 'Newsreader Variable', 'Newsreader', Georgia, serif;
    font-size: clamp(1.5rem, 2.5vw, 2rem);
    font-weight: 400;
    color: var(--text);
    line-height: 1.2;
  }

  .photo-invite__body {
    font-family: 'Barlow', system-ui, sans-serif;
    font-size: 1.0625rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--text);
  }

  .btn-primary {
    display: inline-block;
    align-self: flex-start;
    background-color: var(--crimson);
    color: var(--text-invert);
    font-family: 'Barlow', system-ui, sans-serif;
    font-size: 1rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    padding: 0.75rem 1.75rem;
    border-radius: var(--r);
    text-decoration: none;
    margin-top: 0.5rem;
    transition: background-color 150ms ease;
  }

  .btn-primary:hover,
  .btn-primary:focus-visible {
    background-color: var(--crimson-deep);
  }

  /* ── Responsive ─────────────────────────────────────────── */
  @media (max-width: 700px) {
    .decade {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      gap: 0.5rem;
    }

    .decade__marker {
      flex-direction: row;
      align-items: center;
      gap: 1rem;
    }

    .decade__rule {
      width: 100%;
      height: 2px;
      flex: 1;
      min-height: auto;
    }

    .decade__images-grid {
      flex-direction: column;
    }
  }

  @media (max-width: 400px) {
    .story-body {
      padding-inline: 1rem;
      padding-block: 2.5rem;
    }

    .story-hero__inner {
      padding-inline: 1rem;
    }
  }
</style>
